Here is the memoized version in Python using a dictionary to store already-computed results:

```python
def fib(n, memo={}):
    if n in memo:
        return memo[n]
    if n <= 1:
        return n

    memo[n] = fib(n - 1, memo) + fib(n - 2, memo)
    return memo[n]
```

*(Note: Using a mutable default argument like `memo={}` is fine here since you want the cache to persist across calls, though if you want a fresh cache per top-level call, initialize `memo=None` and set `memo = {}` inside the function or use a wrapper.)*

If you are using Python 3.9+, you can also use the built-in `@cache` decorator to do the exact same thing automatically:

```python
from functools import cache

@cache
def fib(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return n
    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)
```

This brings the time complexity down from $O(2^n)$ to $O(n)$, so `fib(40)` will run almost instantaneously. Let me know if you need this in a different language.

